<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Malava republic</h1><p class="st-meta">330 BCE – 350 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A warrior republic that fought Alexander in the Punjab, migrated to Rajasthan and gave its name to the region of Malwa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Malavas first appear as the Malloi of Greek historians, a republican people of the lower <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> who resisted Alexander in 326 to 325 BCE; Alexander was badly wounded storming one of their towns. In the following centuries they moved south-east into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajasthan">Rajasthan</a>, where they were established near <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nagar">Nagar</a> in the Tonk district by the 2nd century BCE.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Malavas were a gana, governed by an assembly of clan leaders rather than a king. Thousands of small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> coins found at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/nagar">Nagar</a> carry the legend Malavanam jaya, victory of the Malavas. An era beginning in 57 BCE, later known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikram-samvat">Vikrama Samvat</a>, was long called the era of the Malava gana, and it is still used in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Malavas fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/western-satraps">Western Satraps</a> and in the 4th century acknowledged the overlordship of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samudragupta">Samudragupta</a>, whose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prayagraj">Allahabad</a> pillar inscription lists them among subject peoples. They then disappear as an independent power, but the region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> preserves their name.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="republic, 330 BCE – 350 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 38 L50 20 L80 38 Z"/><path d="M24 42 H76 M24 80 H76"/><path d="M30 44 V78 M43 44 V78 M57 44 V78 M70 44 V78"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">republic</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:53.13%;width:13.53%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>330 BCE – 350 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Vedic religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a>, <a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Malavas, Malava Gana</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=10&amp;focus=malava-republic" aria-label="Malava republic on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6815 1306 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Malava republic lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5548 987h0M7362 1550h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7135 1562h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M7135 1562h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day India. <a href="/atlas?year=10&amp;focus=malava-republic">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

